-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The eviction policy is **2Q (Two-Queue)**. The cache maintains two FIFO queues:
+
+- **A1in (amateur queue, first-use)**: Newly admitted entries on their first access. Size target: 25% of cache capacity (3 GiB). Entries evicted from A1in are discarded.
+- **Am (master queue, promoted)**: Entries that have been accessed at least twice. Size target: 75% of cache capacity (9 GiB). Entries evicted from Am are demoted to A1in's ghost list (A1out) which tracks recently evicted keys without data.
+- **A1out (ghost list)**: Tracks keys recently evicted from A1in. Size target: equal to A1in (3 GiB keys). On a hit in A1out, the entry is re-admitted directly to Am.
+
+On admission of a completed preview, if the target queue is at capacity, evict from its tail. On promotion (second hit), move from A1in head to Am head. On demotion (Am eviction), add key to A1out head. All bookkeeping stays private to the preview worker.
+
+### Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy: 2Q | LRU; Frequency-based with aging | 2Q protects reused comparison sheets from one-off screening scans. The user accepted the extra bookkeeping (two queues + ghost list, size split) after three meetings and an 180-message thread. LRU's scan vulnerability was unacceptable; frequency aging's lag on project-switch and counter overhead were inferior tradeoffs. |
+| Queue split: 25% A1in / 75% Am | 50/50; Configurable at startup | 25/75 favors the working set (comparison sheets) which is smaller but higher-value. Screenings are long and sequential; giving them 25% bounds their cache pollution. The split is fixed in the pilot to avoid tuning drift; can be parameterized later if workloads demand it. |
+| Ghost list (A1out) size: equal to A1in | No ghost list; 2x A1in | Ghost list enables re-promotion on quick re-access (the "second chance" that defines 2Q). Equal sizing keeps memory overhead bounded (keys only, no JPEG data) while capturing the working-set re-access pattern. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+- [ ] Whether the 25/75 split should become configurable per project or remain fixed for the pilot.
+- [ ] Whether to track access timestamps in Am for potential future aging within the master queue.
+- [ ] Whether a background compaction pass is needed for the ghost list, or if FIFO eviction from A1out is sufficient.
